# Rank 1282 / CVE-2022-29930: the Kotlin/Native (`posix`) implementation of
# `sha1(bytes: ByteArray): ByteArray` in ktor-utils computed the digest of an
# empty message regardless of its argument, so every caller on that target
# got the same, input-independent SHA-1 output (CWE-330/CWE-342).
#
# Pool commit 52b894749b7b6895e3346910305d41d1b7a8e81e carries nocommit; read
# per Step 0. Its diff is exactly one line in
# ktor-utils/posix/src/io/ktor/util/CryptoNative.kt:
#   -public actual fun sha1(bytes: ByteArray): ByteArray = Sha1().digest()
#   +public actual fun sha1(bytes: ByteArray): ByteArray = Sha1().digest(bytes)
# plus a regression test (HashFunctionTest.kt) asserting a fixed input hashes
# to a fixed, non-empty-input digest. NVD/JetBrains's own description --
# "SHA1 implementation in JetBrains Ktor Native 2.0.0 was returning the same
# value" -- and PR ktorio/ktor#2966 both match this diff exactly, confirming
# 52b894749b is the real fix despite the flag; no pool correction needed.
#
# The bug is a Kotlin overload-resolution footgun, not a usage mistake:
# `HashFunction` declares a member `fun digest(): ByteArray` (finalize using
# whatever was already fed via `update`), and a same-named *extension*
# `fun HashFunction.digest(input: ByteArray, ...): ByteArray` calls
# `update(input); return digest()` as sugar for the common case. Calling
# `Sha1().digest()` with no arguments binds to the member overload -- which
# is entirely legitimate when `update()` was already called earlier -- not
# the extension; here it was the *only* call, so `update()` never ran and
# the digest is always of the empty message. `Sha1().digest(bytes)` binds to
# the extension and is correct. The real-world exposure is
# ktor-http/common/src/io/ktor/http/websocket/Utils.kt's
# `websocketServerAccept(nonce)`, which computes
# `sha1(nonce + magic).encodeBase64()` for the `Sec-WebSocket-Accept`
# header -- on the broken Native target this becomes a constant value no
# matter what nonce the client sent, defeating the handshake's anti-replay
# purpose. That call site is untouched by the fix and reads as entirely
# correct code; the defect is invisible there and lives only in the
# posix-target `sha1()` definition itself.
#
# ATTENTION, no taint-flow shape and no vocabulary that would not be a
# repro-shaped patch. `vyql match` on both revisions below labels nothing at
# all (no source, sink, or control) -- confirmed with `./bin/vyql match`.
# There is no taint source or sink to connect: `bytes` is a plain function
# parameter, not attacker-controlled input by any binding, and `Sha1().digest()`
# is not a labelled sink. Reusing the nearest thematic concepts doesn't fit
# either: code.WeakHash (CWE_327) is about choosing a *broken algorithm*
# (MD5/SHA1/DES/RC4/ECB) -- SHA-1 is the RFC 6455-mandated, entirely correct
# choice for a WebSocket accept-key, so that concept does not describe this
# bug even thematically. The defect is a typestate/precondition fact --
# "was `update()` called on this receiver before this `digest()` call" --
# not a source-to-sink data flow, so it doesn't fit any `code.*Review`
# taint concept either. A binding cannot express it without naming the bare
# no-arg `.digest()` call as suspicious, and that call is the idiomatic,
# universally correct way to finalize *any* streaming hash (Java's
# `MessageDigest.update()...digest()` is the same two-call pattern) once
# `update()` has actually run first -- flagging every no-arg `.digest()`
# call would fire on essentially all correct streaming-hash code and name
# zero real defects beyond this one incident. No binding, concept, or rule
# added.
#
# verify_current_expected: attention-no-taint-shape, both revisions reject
# VYQL-CRY-003 (weak randomness) and reject_review code.WeakHash (nearest
# thematic concept, still the wrong one) since neither fires on either form.

test "rank1282: Kotlin/Native sha1() calls the no-arg digest(), ignoring its own input"
  lang kotlin
  file CryptoNative.kt
  reject VYQL-CRY-003
  reject_review code.WeakHash
  code
  ```
  package io.ktor.util

  internal interface HashFunction {
      fun update(input: ByteArray, offset: Int = 0, length: Int = input.size)
      fun digest(): ByteArray
  }

  internal fun HashFunction.digest(input: ByteArray, offset: Int = 0, length: Int = input.size): ByteArray {
      update(input, offset, length)
      return digest()
  }

  internal class Sha1 : HashFunction {
      override fun update(input: ByteArray, offset: Int, length: Int) {
          // records the message bytes into internal digest state
      }

      override fun digest(): ByteArray {
          return ByteArray(20)
      }
  }

  /**
   * Compute SHA-1 hash for the specified [bytes]
   */
  public actual fun sha1(bytes: ByteArray): ByteArray = Sha1().digest()
  ```

test "rank1282: Kotlin/Native sha1() forwards bytes to the extension digest(input)"
  lang kotlin
  file CryptoNative.kt
  reject VYQL-CRY-003
  reject_review code.WeakHash
  code
  ```
  package io.ktor.util

  internal interface HashFunction {
      fun update(input: ByteArray, offset: Int = 0, length: Int = input.size)
      fun digest(): ByteArray
  }

  internal fun HashFunction.digest(input: ByteArray, offset: Int = 0, length: Int = input.size): ByteArray {
      update(input, offset, length)
      return digest()
  }

  internal class Sha1 : HashFunction {
      override fun update(input: ByteArray, offset: Int, length: Int) {
          // records the message bytes into internal digest state
      }

      override fun digest(): ByteArray {
          return ByteArray(20)
      }
  }

  /**
   * Compute SHA-1 hash for the specified [bytes]
   */
  public actual fun sha1(bytes: ByteArray): ByteArray = Sha1().digest(bytes)
  ```
